WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 29.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.260
It's May 5th, 2026.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.140
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.020
Let's go.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.400
So imagine getting a whole set of free video lessons that teach you something brand new without

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.240
any homework pressure or confusing words.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.820
That is exactly what Google just released today.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.860
10 complete beginner courses on artificial intelligence and machine learning.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.540
These courses are built for people who have never written a single line of code and you

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.840
can start them right on your phone or laptop.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.020
Machine learning is the part of AI where a computer gets better at a task by studying thousands

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:50.060
or millions of examples instead of following strict rules a program or wrote down.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.840
Think of it like learning to recognize your friend's handwriting after seeing enough

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.640
messy notes.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.960
You start spotting the patterns without anyone spelling out every rule.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.660
The lessons use everyday examples like photos, text messages and simple predictions so the

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.980
ideas feel familiar instead of abstract.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.140
For students this matters right now because AI tools already help brainstorm essays, build

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.440
study guides or edit photos for class projects.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.500
And if you are thinking about future careers, understanding how these systems actually work

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.620
gives you a real edge in fields like game design, healthcare or content creation.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.140
You do not have to finish all 10 courses at once.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:40.880
Just pick the one that sounds most interesting such as how AI understands images or language

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.900
and complete the first short module today.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:51.040
Search for Google AI courses or Google free machine learning courses in your browser and

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.760
the full list appears with direct links to start.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.140
Most of them need only a free Google account and run entirely inside the browser.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.200
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.320
at how machine learning actually learns.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.820
You know how when you play the same video game for hours, you start noticing patterns like

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.160
the boss always attacks after three jumps.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:21.420
Even though no one told you the rule out loud, machine learning works in a similar way, but

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.960
with pictures or words instead of game levels.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.540
First the system is shown thousands or millions of examples.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.220
Pictures label cat, dog, car or whatever the task happens to be.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:39.360
Next it looks for the small differences that show up again and again, like pointy ears usually

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.860
meaning cat or round headlights often meaning car.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.180
It does not understand the words the way you do.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.400
It simply keeps score on which clues appear most often with each label.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.320
Then when you show it a brand new picture it has never seen before, it checks the clues

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.260
it learned and makes its best guess.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.880
If the guess is wrong, the system adjusts its scoring system slightly and tries again with

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.920
more examples, just like tweaking your strategy after losing a round in a game.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.780
Over time those tiny adjustments add up so the computer gets better without anyone rewriting

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.380
its instructions from scratch.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.980
And that is basically what the Google courses will teach you in plain language.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.800
How to give an AI the right kind of examples so it can practice and improve on its own.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.400
So next time someone says machine learning, you can tell them it is just a computer getting

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.920
good at a skill, the same way you get good at a game, by seeing the pattern over and over.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.240
Now let's move to some cool stuff you can actually try today.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.560
New AI tools that turn a short text description into a full picture are causing people to

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.820
download apps far more than regular chatbot updates.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.780
Instead of just asking an AI questions, you can now type something like a cozy reading

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:06.220
nook inside a giant tree at golden hour and get an original image in seconds.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.400
Think of it like having a magic sketchbook that draws whatever scene you describe, even

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.940
if you have zero drawing skills.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:20.060
This is exciting if you like making thumbnails for videos, designing characters for stories,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.640
or just messing around with art ideas.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.740
To try it right now, open any app store on your phone and search for AI Image Generator

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.200
or AI Art.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.000
Pick a free app that lets you start creating immediately, type a simple description and

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:37.840
hit generate.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:44.160
Then change one word in your description, for example swap golden hour for rainy night,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.000
and watch how the picture shifts completely.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:53.340
Spend five minutes making three different versions of the same idea, and you will quickly see how

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.180
small changes in your words create big differences in the result.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.280
Next up is something that feels like science fiction, but is already in the works.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.840
One of the co-founders of the company that made the room of vacuum cleaner has started

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.940
a new project building small, dog size robot pets meant to keep you company rather than clean

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:15.560
your house.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.620
These robots are designed to move around, react to you, and feel alive without any of the

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.840
feeding, walking, or cleanup that real pets require.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:31.080
Imagine a Tamagache from your childhood, but physical and smart enough to follow you

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.200
around the room like a digital friend.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.580
While the first versions are not available to buy yet, you can search online for familiar

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.960
machines and magic to see early videos and updates.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:49.080
For a quick creative experiment, open any chat bot like chat GPT or Gemini, and describe

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.420
the perfect robot companion you would want, what it looks like, how it moves, and what it

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.220
does when you come home from school.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.260
Ask it to suggest a name and a short story about your first day together, and you will get

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.840
a fun, personalized idea in seconds.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.840
Here are a couple of quick bits to round out the day.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.300
The White House has been talking with companies like OpenAI, Google, and Anthropic about possibly

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.980
reviewing new AI models before they reach the public.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:26.540
The goal is to catch potential problems early, similar to how new medicines or cars get

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.300
safety checks before they are sold.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.900
This could affect which AI tools show up in your favorite apps over the next few years.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:41.400
And finally, AI researcher Ethan Mollick shared that simply telling chat GPT or similar tools

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.680
to act like an expert no longer improves their answers the way it used to.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:52.420
Instead, try giving the AI a clear example of the style or format you want, or break your

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:53.980
request into smaller steps.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.240
It is like showing a friend a picture of the exact look you are going for instead of

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.700
just saying, make it good.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:05.440
The results usually turn out much better for school work or creative projects.

